cast:
  Zofia:
  Ollie:
  Kit:
  Len:
  Alec:
  Grace:
  Ruth:
  Ewa:
  Banda:
  Vale:
  Hugh:
  Emmeline:
  Mara:
  Niamh:
~~~~
numbered-cover
---
Kit: First, the cover, then the index, then—wait. You need the dates, not my numbering.
Ollie: You numbered the cover. That is page zero. You've given the folder a floor below ground.
Zofia: Dunkirk. When I watched it, I kept putting things in the order I saw them. I could do that with the case. Start with what Kit saw.
Kit: Start with it, yes. Date everything else too. I told you last, but that doesn't mean it happened last.
Ollie: Christopher Nolan. Born in Westminster, 30 July 1970; English literature at University College London. I looked him up after you mentioned the film. Not film studies, which was my extremely confident contribution.
Zofia: Dunkirk was 2017. The evacuation in 1940. I did know those were different dates, thanks.
Kit: Memento, 2000. Inception, 2010. Dunkirk, 2017. Your viewing belongs on another line. Wait—our evidence needs that too. Event, record, when we found it.
Zofia: Fine. But if the index has an index, I'm leaving you here.

two-tablets
---
Len: National Service, miss. We were sent to separate the sides and protect the Canal. Neutral, they told us. Bit of a posting for it. [class=dead]
Zofia: This copy says that too. The later unit account. The Historical Accounts Office holds the original; the Association just has a copy.
Len: Later, miss? Somebody had time to check it, then. Not a letter dashed off before parade. [class=dead]
Ollie: What does he remember?
Zofia: Being told Britain and France were neutral. This account repeats it.
Ollie: The checked chronology starts earlier. Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal Company on 26 July 1956. Then Sèvres: the secret British, French and Israeli plan, 22 to 24 October. Israel would attack Egypt; Britain and France would issue an ultimatum and intervene as peacekeepers.
Zofia: Len, they planned the attack and intervention together. That isn't neutral separation. You weren't told about Sèvres.
Len: Sèvres, miss. No, that wasn't in the briefing. Very short briefing, as it turns out. [class=dead]
Ollie: Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October. Ultimatum on the thirtieth; bombing on the thirty-first. Anglo-French paratroopers landed at Port Said on 5 November; commandos on the sixth. Ceasefire on the sixth too.
Zofia: Those are checked dates, Len. I'm telling you now, here. Your account needs the secret plan beside its claim about purpose.
Len: Purpose, miss. Yes. Not a smarter account of my kit. The purpose is the word they must answer. [class=dead]
Zofia: Alec? This board doesn't have your name.
Alec: Doesn't have it yet, you mean. Navy's fond of a list. Get the war finished and they'll find room, unless they've spent it all on the football results. [class=dead]
Zofia: It did finish. Kit has the record. He can't hear you, so I'll ask him.
Zofia: Kit, tell me the surrender date. Alec thinks the war is still going.
Kit: Argentina invaded the Falklands on 2 April 1982. The British task force sailed on 5 April. The surrender at Port Stanley was 14 June: Menéndez to Jeremy Moore. Seventy-four days from invasion to surrender.
Zofia: Alec, 14 June 1982. After you died. The war ended, but this board still doesn't name you.
Alec: Full time, then. And still no name. Lovely. Dai will have decided I pinched his cassette by now. [class=dead]
Kit: The reported deaths are 255 British military personnel, 649 Argentine military personnel and three Falkland Islanders. That's the historical total. It can't identify somebody on this board.
Zofia: Alec, those numbers don't tell us why you're missing. We'll ask about your name separately.
Mara: Zofia, tell him I know what it means to wait for a son who—no. That isn't a promise I can make for him. I can ask them to search.
Zofia: Mara lost her son at sea. She wants them to search for you.
Alec: Sorry for her boy. But I'm not her boy, am I? Different ship, different mum. Tell her Alec will do. No substitute coming off the bench. [class=dead]
Zofia: He says Alec will do. Please don't put him in your son's place.
Mara: Alec. I'll use his name. I'll get it onto—I'll get the omission investigated.
Zofia: Len, Alec, will you come with me? There are two requests. I'm keeping them separate.
Len: Two files, miss. Saves the orderly room losing us both in one envelope. [class=dead]
Alec: Aye, I'll come. Keep his briefing out of my cassette case, though. [class=dead]

separate-wars
---
Zofia: One statement might get more attention. Both wars, everyone left out—
Len: Attention, miss? Had plenty of that at inspection. I want the purpose corrected. [class=dead]
Alec: And I want my name checked, not a place in somebody's all-star side. [class=dead]
Zofia: They both hate it. Len wants the purpose corrected. Alec wants the missing name investigated.
Grace: Then why are you trying to make them sign the same sentence?
Zofia: Because I've got one bag and about ten minutes. All right. Separate sleeves.
Ollie: Suez pressure in Len's sleeve. American financial and diplomatic pressure, including the threat to sterling; UN and Soviet pressure too. Britain couldn't carry on independently of the United States. That wrecks the account's neat little rescue story, but it doesn't prove anything about Alec.
Kit: Then Eden resigned on 9 January 1957; Macmillan succeeded him. Not a reason to write that every person in Egypt was just scenery for a British downfall.
Grace: Egyptian people lived where the bombing happened. Put that where somebody can see it.
Ollie: I have. Egyptian military deaths are estimated at about 1,650 to 3,000. The Canal reopened in spring 1957. The crisis exposed the limits of British imperial power. That doesn't turn the people killed into a diagram of Britain's importance.
Kit: Alec's sleeve has the long Argentine sovereignty claim and the military junta's invasion. UN Resolution 502 demanded Argentine withdrawal. The islanders lived with the consequences; they weren't a prize on a scoreboard.
Ollie: After defeat the Argentine military government fell and democracy returned. Thatcher's government won a larger majority in 1983. Those consequences matter. They still won't supply the missing identity link.
Zofia: Put the history sheets behind each request. The rota goes at the front of Ruth's packet. If we miss her, neither sleeve gets into that room with us.

signed-copy
---
Ruth: Zofia, originals on the desk. Kit, remain available. Ewa, identify your signature.
Ewa: My signature. Week four, Tuesday. Six until ten. I finished the shift and left. Your summary puts me there until six in the evening.
Zofia: The 14:40. I thought it tied everything together. It ties the wrong weeks together.
Banda: Week two: I logged the visitor at 14:10. Your copy has 14:40. Ewa left the room empty that day. Week four: she went home at ten. I saw Vale at both doors that afternoon.
Ruth: Samuel, I will release the original log with both copies.
Banda: Give the officers my name with it. I saw the contractor enter and leave. Zofia did not bring him through either door.
Ruth: That will expose the failures in the family access arrangement. I cannot continue sponsoring it.
Banda: Then it ends. My children do not owe you an allegation against somebody else's mother to keep their visits.
Ewa: The rota is mine. Keep my name with it when you send it on. I am not leaving my job because you copied a shift.
Ruth: You are not being removed. I am signing the correction to the allegation against both of you. You assembled the evidence that disproves it.
Zofia: And the missing Mace? The thing you kept calling reconciliation?
Ruth: I kept it quiet. That protected me.
Ewa: Send that sentence with the correction.
Ruth: I will. My handling of this goes for scrutiny with it.

hearing-not-price
---
Grace: Mr Vale, an independent examination of your mother's work. Evidence, reasons, your objection kept with the answer. Will you accept that?
Vale: If the answer says she was the sole rescuer. Not helped. Not contributed. Sole.
Hugh: Her contribution deserves examination. My father's account does not become a second source because another writer copied it. I was wrong to treat it as corroboration.
Vale: Then correct it. Before I tell you anything.
Hugh: I would preserve that account beside the evidence against it. The Monument's anti-Catholic accusation was finally chiselled out in 1830; the removal is itself part of the record. A change must show what was changed and why. Your preferred account cannot be allowed to hide its own making either.
Zofia: I wanted a clean page too. I was wrong. Keep the bad one where we can see who used it.
Vale: My father got a hearing. He got a tribute. I want a correction.
Grace: So do I. A real one requires checking. You can't make us pay your chosen verdict for the chance to ask about our own records.
Ruth: Mr Vale, officers will speak to you now. Children, return to the waiting area with Samuel. Nobody follows the case.
Ollie: Dad, wait. I told Vale the route. And that it had to be the genuine Mace. I told a journalist too, after Zofia's trial. Vale heard it from me. Not from you. I'll give the names and when I said it.
Banda: You will give that account to the officers, with me present. Then we will go home when we are released.
Grace: The Mace again. But why do we still do this? If we're allowed in only while nobody is embarrassed, whose ceremony am I defending?
Hugh: Because this sitting is where I have committed myself publicly to contesting that account. I cannot offer the same accountability by telling you to trust me in a corridor.
Grace: Then put Ewa and Dad's accounts beside yours. People outside this corridor should hear them too.

unfinished-instruction
---
Emmeline: We sent letters; now we must bring people. Let Mara call a public meeting. Let the women waiting for answers fill the chairs, and let the Office explain its delay to them. [class=dead]
Zofia: There's a reply. From the Workshop editor. Your caption.
Emmeline: Read it aloud. We did not campaign for the vote to leave women's lives in somebody else's careless order. [class=dead]
Zofia: Emmeline, I'm reading the signed correction above. “We retract that sequence.” They've put your death first. June, then July.
Zofia: Can I show Mara your page?
Emmeline: Yes. Take it to the meeting. We have won this correction; now bring the women still waiting to— [class=dead]
Zofia: To what?
Zofia: Please. The next office to what?
Kit: Zofia? Our escort's here. Wait—do you need a minute?
Zofia: She stopped. In the middle.
Kit: We can ask for a minute. I can't promise more.
Zofia: No. I'll take the page.

after-response
---
Mara: Give me the refusal. I'll demand they reverse it.
Hugh: On which evidence? Read the damaged-list paragraph first.
Zofia: Alec, I'm reading the secretary's signed decision here. There's a fragment with your name. Is there anything else you remember about that board?
Alec: A fragment? Navy managed all those forms and I'm down to a bit of one? [class=dead]
Zofia: “Not established.” They won't add it. They haven't said you didn't serve, Alec. Just that they can't join it up.
Alec: Not established. That's a rotten result to take home after extra time. I still had Dai's tape in my locker— [class=dead]
Zofia: Alec?
Zofia: I believe you. I can't put your name there myself.
Mara: What did he say?
Zofia: His friend's tape. He hadn't taken it back. Then nothing.
Mara: I owe him a search that stays open to new evidence. I had my pen out to promise his name again.
Len: Nothing for the unit account, miss? Still waiting outside the orderly room? [class=dead]
Zofia: They said pending policy. No search, no reasons about your purpose. That isn't your answer, Len.
Len: Pending, miss. Best not write “answered” against my name, then. [class=dead]

return-the-pass
---
Kit: Separately scheduled business. The Records (Correction) Bill. First reading—that's introduction. Not passed. Wait, I wanted to say that before somebody clapped.
Mara: Len and Hale still have no evidence-based reply. I'm seeking a duty to search and give reasons. Today's response hasn't done that for them.
Zofia: The pass. Do I have to hand it back now?
Kit: Yes. This was the last escorted visit under the arrangement.
Zofia: Then take it before I lose it in all this paper.
Niamh: My mum's application came back. Belfast on the first page, wrong body on the envelope. Who sent it there?
Zofia: We thought the UK label would—
Niamh: I gave you my appointment. I want it back.
